From: =?UTF-8?q?Ant=C3=B3nio=20Sarmento?= <Keyaku@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 23:33:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] SurfaceFlinger: publish the external display's power state

The HDMI daemon has no event-driven signal for "the device went to sleep with a monitor attached": nothing in the framework flips a property on sleep, and polling costs wakeups for the whole uptime. setPowerModeInternal is the one place that sees every power change on the external, so it publishes it as sys.xdplus.hdmi_power (0 asleep, 1 awake). The daemon sleeps on that property and switches the HDMI output off after a user-set timeout, back on at wake.

Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I8bedfc764bdd22746fbe0fce806abe797b8c622b
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index 50115b3543..49a97dfe63 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -4373,6 +4373,12 @@ void SurfaceFlinger::setPowerModeInternal(const sp<DisplayDevice>& display, hal:
         mScheduler->setDisplayPowerState(mode == hal::PowerMode::ON);
     }
 
+    // The only event-driven signal the HDMI daemon has for a sleep timeout:
+    // it tears the external down after the timeout and rebuilds it on wake.
+    if (!display->isPrimary()) {
+        property_set("sys.xdplus.hdmi_power", mode == hal::PowerMode::OFF ? "0" : "1");
+    }
+
     ALOGD("Finished setting power mode %d on display %s", mode, to_string(*displayId).c_str());
 }
 
